  "readmeFile": "README.md",
  "releaseNotes": "NEW\n\t\t\t* Using<T>(setup) and Using<T>(transform) arrange every generated value of a type,\n\t\t\t  taking the same optional scope as any Using arrangement — For.Input, For.Subject or\n\t\t\t  For.All (default). Reads \"Using Room with BedCount = 2 for Subject\". Out of scope, a\n\t\t\t  setup neither applies nor supplies a default.\n\n\t\t\tCHANGED\n\t\t\t* A value arranged through an article — Given().A<T>(setup), .ASecond, .Two, etc. — reads\n\t\t\t  as the noun phrase it is: \"a Room with BedCount = 2\", \"two Rooms with BedCount = 2\".\n\n\t\t\tOBSOLETE\n\t\t\t* Given<T>(setup), Given<T>(transform) and the matching And overloads — use\n\t\t\t  Using<T>(...) instead. Behaviour is unchanged, and both spellings render alike.\n\t\t\t  Note that continuing a chain after Using reaches the Using vocabulary, so a following\n\t\t\t  mock setup is spelled .Given<TService>() rather than .And<TService>().",
